From: vincek@intergalact.com (Vince Kerchner)
Date: Fri, 9 Dec 1994 01:22:12 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-361] SING: Are we still evolving?

Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com> sez:

>Huh?  Selection is death.

That's right.  Selection means killing off those genotypes for which the
corresponding phenotypes are not suitably adapted for survival in the
current environment.  Those phenotypes which remain can then reproduce to
create additional individuals carrying their parent's gene line.  In
general, genotypes are not selected "for", they are selected "against",
meaning that their phenotypes do not live to reproduce.  Phenotypes which
survive may reproduce at different rates, but that is a matter of
fecundity, not selection.

>Is technology really our friend, is evolution over finally?

Well, hopefully technology is our friend, but I suppose that depends on how
we use it.  As for evolution, I think that is still alive and well.

Evolution consists to two main processes: mutation and selection.  Mutation
(or equivalent processes) alters the gene line by changing the sequence of
bases (base-pairs in eucaryotes) of DNA (genotype), which in turn determine
the sequence of amino acids in proteins, which in turn regulate biochemical
reactions in the cell, which in turn (a big, and relatively poorly
understood, "in turn") determine the physical properties (phenotype) of the
organism.  These physical properties determine how well the organism will
fare in its environment, and whether it will live to the age of
reproduction.

Reproduction simply determines how many more individuals containing the
same gene-set will be produced; it does not alter that gene-set.
Reproduction does, however, change the relative frequency of a particular
gene-set within a given population.  Organisms which breed more
prodigiously than others will increase the relative frequency of their
gene-sets, so that one gene-set may therefore "out-evolve" another,
competing, gene-set over time.

Presumably, mutation is still occurring at a rate similar to that which it
has for millions of years.  In fact, some poeple worry that the mutation
rate is increasing due to human interaction with the biosphere in terms of
ionizing radiation from the mining, processing, and use of heavy isotopes,
atmospheric ozone depletion and concomitant increases in ultraviolet solar
radiation, and the release of hydrocarbons and other chemicals into the
atmosphere, water, and food supply.  At present, we have very little
control over mutation.

We are, however, significantly reducing the selective pressures which would
otherwise eliminate gene-sets from the population.  In general, the use of
technology makes our lives safer and easier, and therefore allows
individuals not fully suited to their environments to live and reproduce.
Examples of this are as simple as central heating in winter, or as
complicated as genetically engineered medicines to cure fatal and
debilitating diseases.  Social policy also reduces selective pressures, by
distributing necessary resources to individuals who would otherwise not be
self-sufficient.

Whether these influences on selective pressure are good or bad is probably
a matter of opinion.  However, evolutionary theory and paleontological
evidence suggest that (a) populations with a greater genetic diversity are
"fitter": they are better able to adapt to varying conditions, and are more
resistant to environmental changes, and (b) populations which cease to
evolve are soon (in evolutionary timescales) wiped out by natural
disasters, the emergence of recessive but fatal genes, or are out-bred by
competing populations.

It is interesting to note that there is still quite a bit of "room" for
human evolution, genetically speaking.  There are 4 different DNA bases
used to encode genetic sequences, and these bases are used in groups of 3
to encode each amino acid in a protein.  This gives a possible 4 x 4 x 4,
or 64, combinations for different amino acid sequences.  However, proteins
are built from a set of only 20 basic amino acids.  This gives a genetic
"headroom" of 44 / 20, or 220%, potentially new amino acids for use in
protein construction.  I have no idea what these new compounds would be, or
how they would work, but I am confident that they will eventually occur,
given time.

--Vince

