> Anarchy means "without rulers". Your definition is simply a common
> misconception.

Geoff and Vince disagree about "the" meaning of "anarchy". Since it is what 
political theorists call an "essentially contested concept" I'm not 
surprised. The dictionary defines it as (1) the absence of any form of 
governmental authority or law, and (2) disorder and confusion. Since this 
definition, obviously written by a statist, conflates law with government,
if we were to accept it, we couldn't say yes or no to the question "are you
an anarchist?". Free market anarchism, in the Rothbard-Friedman conception
has laws, but no monopoly government making all the laws. 

Since the dictionary definition doesn't make much sense to us, we need to 
redefine it to mean "the absence of any monopoly enforcer of law" or similar.
There's nothing to prevent Vince using it to mean "without rules or 
restrictions", but such a definition seems unhelpful for us. I can't quite go
along with Geoff in calling Vince's definition a common "misconception",
since he and others can use the word to mean that if they wish, though 
"chaos" seems to fit that definition already. Nevertheless, I essentially
agree with Geoff on this.

> I'd have to check with Max, Tom and other early extropians, since I wasn't
> there in the beginning, but I'm pretty damn sure Anarcho-Capitalism has
> been inextricably linked to extropianism from the get-go. It certainly has
> since I've been here.

I'm not sure how Geoff and Vince disagree here. Vince makes sense in saying
that Extropianism opposes any form of government that interferes with 
boundless expansion and our other goals. Geoff is correct that Anarcho-
Capitalism had been linked to Extropianism from the start. Geoff's use of
"inextricably" linked makes me think Vince is closer to the mark. 
Extropianism does not advocate any specific form of social system. It 
stresses certain values and goals, including spontaneous order. Any social
and economic system that turned out to accord with these principles and
values would be consonant with Extropianism. 

Most of us suspect that the system most conducive to extropic ends will be
A-C/free market anarchism (or, as Tom and I prefer, polycentric law). I 
think it a mistake to insist that Extropianism is tied to a particular 
political position. It might just turn out that some other arrangement, such
as a minimal state run by incorruptible synthetic intelligences, would 
be more extropic. For the same reason, I think it mistaken to insist that,
before someone can call themselves an Extropian, they must agree to the
feasibility of cryonics and support it. Supporting life extension by any
effective means forms a core part of being extropian, but not the belief
in or support of any specific technology. I see social technologies such
as political systems as having the same status.

> On the subject of the Principles:
> 
> Every time I've tried to introduce someone to the extropian movement with
> the principles, their eyes glaze over and they don't get it. For the book,
> we may use a version that doesn't have such stiff sounding names (for the
> principles) and flowery prose. (Don't get me wrong, they work great on 
> well educated proto-extropians, but that isn't necessarily our target
> audience).

Maybe you're not doing it right! I haven't noticed this response, though
perhaps I've only spoken to "well-educated proto-extropians". I think
the trick is not to present the Principles alone in the abstract, but to 
immediately relate each to various implementations and possibilities
(without *defining* the principles in terms of the specifics). Or, you
can explain some of the extropian goals, such as living indefinitely,
augmenting intellect, becoming physically superior, etc., then show how 
these goals form commonalities expressed by the Principles. I start
by explaining "Extropy" since it ties together all the other ideas neatly.

